  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Graphical variation of electric field due to a uniformly charged insulating solid sphere of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo></math>with distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></math> from the centre <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> is represented by:</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0080_0b4b11faebbc.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 191px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<p>Dig&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"assets\\ot1_0080_913dc656da02.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 148px;\" /></p>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\ot2_0080_8c01dba2b183.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 147px;\" /></p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\ot3_0080_4c5f80a73aea.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 146px;\" /></p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\ot4_0080_2aaefe7af366.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 153px;\" /></p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The electric field inside a solid insulated sphere is given by&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math>&nbsp;for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;volume&nbsp;charge density.</p><p>Hence,&nbsp;the electric field varies linearly with the distance as</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mi>inside</mi></msub><mo>&#8733;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></math></p><p>Now, the electric field for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;is given by&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mi>outside</mi></msub><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math></p><p>Thus, the graph starts decreasing as a power law graph as shown in the figure for the region&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>.</p><p>Hence, this graph is the right option.</p></blockquote>"
  }
